/-!
# Stochastic Accelerated Gradient Descent

Statement-only formalization of Lan's generic composite AC-SA method from
Section 4.2 of *First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine
Learning*. The file packages the composite objective, Bregman geometry,
stochastic oracle assumptions, and accelerated recursion into a single setup,
then declares the main lemma/proposition/theorem chain with proof bodies
replaced by `sorry`.
-/

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open SOptLib
open Filter
open scoped InnerProductSpace BigOperators Topology

variable {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
  [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]

variable {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S]
variable {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- State of AC-SA at time `t`: the prox iterate `x_t` and accelerated average
`x̄_t`. The search point `x̲_t` is derived from the previous state and schedule. -/
structure StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentState (E : Type*) where
  x : E
  xBar : E

/-- Feasible AC-SA state over the paper carrier `X`. This is the state actually
used by the recursive process so the update rule cannot leave the paper domain
silently. -/
abbrev FeasibleStochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentState
    (X : Set E) : Type _ :=
  SOptLib.FeasibleAcceleratedState X

/-- Structural measurable space on feasible AC-SA states, generated by the two
ambient iterate coordinates. The feasibility certificates are proof-only fields,
so the stochastic recursion's measurability is exactly measurability of
`(x_t, xbar_t)`.

This is realization infrastructure rather than a paper assumption: it lets the
sample-driven kernel for Eq. (4.2.6) compose with Mathlib/SOptLib measurable
recursion APIs. -/
instance feasibleStateMeasurableSpace (X : Set E) :
    MeasurableSpace (FeasibleStochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentState (E := E) X) :=
  SOptLib.feasibleAcceleratedStateMeasurableSpace X

/-- The coordinate map generating the feasible AC-SA state measurable space is
measurable by the defining `MeasurableSpace.comap`.

This local infrastructure lemma aligns with the feasible-state instance above;
the SOptLib candidates considered for the selected measurability goals were
recursive-process and product-coordinate replacement lemmas, but none exposes
this paper-local two-coordinate feasible-state comap directly. -/
private theorem feasible_state_coordinates_measurable (X : Set E) :
    Measurable
      (fun s : FeasibleStochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentState (E := E) X =>
        (s.x, s.xBar)) := by
  exact Measurable.of_comap_le le_rfl

/-- Internal recursion carrier for the AC-SA weight schedule `Γ_t` from
Eq. (4.2.21), constructed from the paper step weights `α_t`.

The paper-facing schedule below is indexed by positive times. This total
function has a harmless `0` branch only so Lean can define the recursive
equation structurally; `Γ_0` is not exported as paper semantics.

No SOptLib match: searched `recursive parameter schedule Gamma alpha`, checked
the pre-search digest, and scanned `SOptLib/Model/ParameterChoices.lean` and
`SOptLib/Model/Iterates.lean`; those files contain closed-form budget and
weighted-output helpers, not this paper's recursive Eq. (4.2.21) schedule. -/
def acsaGamma (alpha : ℕ → ℝ) : ℕ → ℝ
  | 0 => 1
  | 1 => 1
  | t + 2 => (1 - alpha (t + 2)) * acsaGamma alpha (t + 1)

/-- Domain-checked quotient helper for source formulas whose mathematical
meaning requires a nonzero denominator.

This is an internal realization-boundary helper, not a paper object: it keeps
the denominator witness visible at every use instead of letting a paper-facing
declaration rely on Lean's total `/` outside the source domain. -/
noncomputable def acsaCheckedQuotient (num den : ℝ) (_ : den ≠ 0) : ℝ :=
  num / den

/-- AC-SA prox objective formula from Eq. (4.2.6), abstracted over the paper
geometry and simple term.

No SOptLib match: searched `prox argmin mirror descent Bregman` and
`composite prox argmin simple convex solvability`, checked `SOptLib.proxStep`,
`SOptLib.IsPaperProxPoint`, `SOptLib.proxPoint`, and
`SOptLib.compositeProxPoint_exists_of_selector`, and scanned
`SOptLib/Model/Prox.lean`; those candidates cover `⟪g,u⟫ + γ⁻¹ V(x,u)+h(u)`
or `γ⟪g,u⟫+V(x,u)`, not the literal Eq. (4.2.6) objective with both
`γ μ V(x̲,u)` and `V(x_{t-1},u)`. -/
noncomputable def acsaProxObjective
    (gamma : ℕ → ℝ) (mu : ℝ) (h : E → ℝ) (V : E → E → ℝ)
    (t : ℕ) (xPrev xUnder g x : E) : ℝ :=
  SOptLib.acceleratedCompositeProxObjective gamma mu h V id t xPrev xUnder g x

/-- Literal Bregman divergence formula used by AC-SA in Eq. (3.2.2).

The candidate `bregmanDivergence` was checked and aligns at the formula level,
but this paper keeps an explicit gradient field `gradNu`; the local definition
prevents the canonical prox objective from depending on Mathlib's ambient `∇`
choice. -/
def acsaBregman (nu : E → ℝ) (gradNu : E → E) (x z : E) : ℝ :=
  nu z - nu x - ⟪gradNu x, z - x⟫_ℝ

/-- Paper-local expression grammar for the quoted "simple" term `h`.

The source does not define "simple" as an arbitrary convex function: it says the
term has known structure, with examples such as `0` and `‖·‖₁`.  This small
grammar records that Lean-facing known structure as data, so carrier Borel
measurability is derived from concrete continuous primitives instead of being
stored as a standalone setup assumption.

No SOptLib match: searched `simple convex term measurable`, `composite
objective simple term`, and scanned `SOptLib/Model/Objective.lean`,
`SOptLib/Model/Carrier.lean`, plus the sister-paper `SimpleConvexTerm` in
`Algorithms/Unverified/NonconvexStochasticMirrorDescent.lean`; those expose
generic carrier convexity/null-measurability, not an AC-SA simple-term
known-structure expression with an ambient evaluator. -/
abbrev AcsSimpleTermExpr (E : Type*) [NormedAddCommGroup E]
    [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr E

namespace AcsSimpleTermExpr

abbrev const (c : ℝ) : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.const c

abbrev norm : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.norm

abbrev affine (g : E) (c : ℝ) : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.affine g c

abbrev add (e₁ e₂ : AcsSimpleTermExpr E) : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.add e₁ e₂

abbrev smul (c : ℝ) (e : AcsSimpleTermExpr E) : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.smul c e

abbrev max (e₁ e₂ : AcsSimpleTermExpr E) : AcsSimpleTermExpr E :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.max e₁ e₂

/-- Evaluation of the AC-SA simple-term expression in the ambient space. -/
noncomputable abbrev eval : AcsSimpleTermExpr E → E → ℝ :=
  SimpleConvexTermExpr.eval

/-- Every expression generated by the paper-local simple-term grammar is
ambient-continuous. -/
theorem continuous : ∀ e : AcsSimpleTermExpr E, Continuous e.eval := by
  exact SimpleConvexTermExpr.continuous


/-- Every paper-local simple-term expression is globally Lipschitz in the
ambient Hilbert norm.

This is the AC-SA known-structure bridge for `h : X → ℝ simple convex`: checked
the SOptLib bounded-subgradient Lipschitz candidates, but they require a
subgradient selector and uniform norm bound; the expression grammar gives a
direct structural proof for the literal simple terms used in Lan §4.2. -/
theorem exists_lipschitz_eval (e : AcsSimpleTermExpr E) :
    ∃ K : ℝ, 0 ≤ K ∧ ∀ x y : E, |e.eval x - e.eval y| ≤ K * ‖x - y‖ := by
  exact SimpleConvexTermExpr.exists_lipschitz_eval e

end AcsSimpleTermExpr

/-- Formal paper-facing convexity content of `h : X → ℝ simple convex` used by AC-SA.

The book JSON states `h : X → ℝ simple convex` at
`book/FOML/StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescent.json#/setup/variable_space/math`
and the PDF describes it as having known structure, with examples `h = 0` and
`h = ‖·‖₁`. The source does not define `simple` as all-states solvability of
Eq. (4.2.6), nor as a global Lipschitz or Borel-regularity assumption, so prox
solvability and any analytic regularity are exposed through explicit leaves
below.  The known-structure witness is bookkeeping for the reconstructed object
model; the proof-facing paper content is the quoted `ConvexOn` field.

`SOptLib.compositeProxPoint_exists_of_selector`, `SOptLib.proxStep`, and
`SOptLib.proxObjective` were checked, but their objectives omit AC-SA's
additional `γ_t μ V(x̲_t, ·)` term or use a different inverse-stepsize
normalization, so this paper-local predicate is needed for the literal
Eq. (4.2.6) update. -/
abbrev IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm
    (X : Set E) (h : E → ℝ) :=
  IsSimpleConvexTermOn X h

/-- Convexity component of the paper phrase `h simple convex`. -/
theorem IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm.convex
    {X : Set E} {h : E → ℝ}
    (hsimple : IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm (E := E) X h) :
    ConvexOn ℝ X h :=
  IsSimpleConvexTermOn.convex hsimple

/-- Carrier measurability is not discharged from `h simple convex`.

The paper's phrase "simple convex with known structure" does not state a
general measurability theorem for the ambient Lean realization.  Keeping this as
an open leaf prevents the proof from silently upgrading `simple` into a
source-unstated regularity assumption. -/
theorem IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm.carrier_measurable
    {X : Set E} {h : E → ℝ}
    (hsimple : IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm (E := E) X h) :
    Measurable (fun x : {x : E // x ∈ X} => h x.1) := by
  exact IsSimpleConvexTermOn.carrier_measurable hsimple

/-- Source-facing stochastic first-order oracle interface for AC-SA.

The PDF Section 4.2 describes an SFO which, for any query point in `X`, returns
`G(x, ξ)` with the unbiasedness and bounded-variance identities used in
Assumptions 2 and 3.  The fixed-query fields below are the displayed
expectations; the final field is the Mathlib/SOptLib regularity needed to
sample the same centered oracle at random feasible queries.  Keeping these
together prevents the random-query measurability requirement from appearing as
an isolated Setup axiom while still exposing the reusable SOptLib oracle API
terminal `SOptLib.sampledOracle_measurable`.

SOptLib search audit: checked `SOptLib.oracleWellDefined`,
`SOptLib.oracleMean`, `SOptLib.sampledOracle_measurable`,
`integrable_sq_oracleResidual_of_indep_fixed_variance_bound`, and
`oracleRandomIterateVarianceBound_of_fixedVariance`; SOptLib has the
Mathlib-canonical oracle/variance bridges but not this paper's combined AC-SA
SFO assumption record. -/
abbrev AcsStochasticOracle
    (X : Set E) (sampleMeasure : Measure S)
    (gradL : E → S → E) (gradF : E → E) (sigma : ℝ) :=
  SOptLib.BoundedVarianceUnbiasedOracleOn X sampleMeasure gradL gradF
    (SOptLib.dualNorm (E := E)) sigma

/-- Paper-facing distance-generating-function assumption from Eq. (3.2.2).

The book states that `v : X → ℝ` is continuously differentiable and
1-strongly convex, with `V(x,z)=v(z)-[v(x)+⟪∇v(x),z-x⟫]`. This local name is
retained for paper-facing setup readability; the reusable predicate lives in
`SOptLib.Model.Bregman.IsDistanceGeneratingFunctionOn`. -/
def IsAcsDistanceGeneratingFunction
    (X : Set E) (nu : E → ℝ) (gradNu : E → E) : Prop :=
  IsDistanceGeneratingFunctionOn X nu gradNu

/-- Source-level AC-SA parameter conditions from Eqs. (4.2.9), (4.2.10),
(4.2.22), and the stated datum `α_1 = 1`.

The source-facing setup records all displayed relations from the book. The
SOptLib predicate stores the algebraic form of the displayed relations; this
paper-local predicate also records the non-initial left-denominator domain of
the fractional Eq. (4.2.9). The initial case is deliberately excluded because
`α_1 = 1` makes `α_1(1-q_1)` vanish.

No SOptLib match: searched `parameter schedule Gamma alpha q stochastic
accelerated`, checked the pre-search digest, and scanned
`SOptLib/Model/ParameterChoices.lean` and `SOptLib/Model/Iterates.lean`; those
files provide other papers' closed-form schedules and generic weighted-output
helpers, not Lan's AC-SA coupled `q_t`, `α_t`, `γ_t`, `Γ_t` conditions. -/
def AcsaDisplayedParameterRelations
    (q alpha gamma : ℕ → ℝ) (mu L : ℝ) : Prop :=
  SOptLib.acceleratedParameterRelations
      (SOptLib.acceleratedGammaScheduleCore alpha) q alpha gamma mu L ∧
    ∀ t, 2 ≤ t → alpha t * (1 - q t) ≠ 0

/-- Paper setup for stochastic accelerated gradient descent (generic composite
AC-SA). This structure contains only source-facing mathematical data and stated
assumptions: feasible set, composite objective, Bregman geometry, stochastic
oracle law, schedule data, and the selected optimum. Concrete sample streams,
filtrations, measurability, independence, and law-realization facts live in
`StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentRealization`. -/
structure StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup
    (E : Type*) [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
      [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    (S : Type*) [MeasurableSpace S] where
  /- Paper setup data: `book/FOML/StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescent.json#/setup/variable_space/math`
     quotes `X ⊆ ℝ^m closed convex`. -/
  X : Set E
  /- Paper algorithm data: `#/algorithm_spec/initialization/math` quotes
     `(x_0, \tilde{x}_0) ∈ X × X`. -/
  w0 : E
  wbar0 : E
  /- Paper algorithm parameters: `#/algorithm_spec/steps` and
     `#/algorithm_spec/parameters` quote `q_t`, `α_t`, `γ_t`, and `Γ_t`. -/
  q : ℕ → ℝ
  alpha : ℕ → ℝ
  gamma : ℕ → ℝ
  /- Paper assumption parameters:
     `#/assumptions/0/parameters` quotes `μ ≥ 0`, `L ≥ 0`, `M ≥ 0`;
     `#/assumptions/2/parameters` quotes `σ² ≥ 0`. -/
  mu : ℝ
  L : ℝ
  M : ℝ
  sigma : ℝ
  /- Paper setup/object data: `#/setup/problem/math` quotes `Ψ(x) := f(x) + h(x)`;
     `#/setup/variable_space/math` quotes `h : X → ℝ simple convex`. -/
  h : E → ℝ
  /- Paper distance-generating data: `#/assumptions/3/math` quotes
     `v : X → ℝ` and `V(x,z)=v(z)-[v(x)+⟪∇v(x),z-x⟫]`. -/
  nu : E → ℝ
  gradNu : E → E
  /- Paper oracle/objective data: `#/setup/variable_space/math` quotes
     `f(x)=𝔼[F(x,ξ)]`; `#/assumptions/1/math` quotes
     `𝔼[G(x,ξ)] = f'(x) ∈ ∂ f(x)`. -/
  gradF : E → E
  oracleValue : E → S → ℝ
  gradL : E → S → E
  /- Paper stochastic sample law: `#/setup/variable_space/math` quotes
     `f(x)=𝔼[F(x,ξ)]`, and `#/assumptions/1/math` quotes
     `𝔼[G(x,ξ)] = f'(x)`. -/
  sampleMeasure : Measure S
  hsampleMeasure : IsProbabilityMeasure sampleMeasure
  /- Paper-facing setup properties from `#/setup/variable_space/math`:
     `X ⊆ ℝ^m closed convex`. -/
  hX_closed : IsClosed X
  hX_convex : Convex ℝ X
  /- Paper-facing initialization properties from `#/algorithm_spec/initialization/math`:
     `(x_0, \tilde{x}_0) ∈ X × X`. -/
  hw0_mem : w0 ∈ X
  hwbar0_mem : wbar0 ∈ X
  /- Paper-facing parameter signs from `#/assumptions/0/parameters` and
     `#/assumptions/2/parameters`: `μ ≥ 0`, `L ≥ 0`, `M ≥ 0`, `σ² ≥ 0`. -/
  hmu_nonneg : 0 ≤ mu
  hL_nonneg : 0 ≤ L
  hM_nonneg : 0 ≤ M
  /- Paper-facing parameter conditions from `#/algorithm_spec/steps` and
     `#/assumptions/4/math`, quoting Eqs. (4.2.9), (4.2.10), (4.2.22), and
     `α_1 = 1`. Extra interval, positivity, and denominator facts are not stored
     here because the source does not state them as primitive Theorem 4.4
     assumptions. -/
  hparams : AcsaDisplayedParameterRelations q alpha gamma mu L
  /- Paper-facing distance-generating assumption from `#/assumptions/3/math`:
     `v` is continuously differentiable and 1-strongly convex, with Bregman
     distance defined by Eq. (3.2.2). -/
  hnu_dgf : IsAcsDistanceGeneratingFunction (E := E) X nu gradNu
  /- Paper-facing setup property from `#/setup/variable_space/math`: `h` is
     `simple convex`. This is modeled only by the quote-backed convexity
     component; Eq. (4.2.6) solvability is a theorem obligation below. -/
  hh_simple : IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm (E := E) X h
  /- Paper setup datum `#/setup/variable_space/math`, quoting
     `f(x)=𝔼[F(x,ξ)]`: the expected stochastic objective is well-defined on
     feasible points. -/
  hobjective_wellDefined :
    ∀ x, x ∈ X → SOptLib.objectiveWellDefined sampleMeasure oracleValue id x
  /- Paper-facing oracle subgradient part of `#/assumptions/1/math`, quoting
     `f'(x) ∈ ∂ f(x)`. -/
  hgradF_subgrad :
    ∀ x (hx : x ∈ X),
      gradF x ∈
        SOptLib.carrierSubdifferential
          (fun y : {y : E // y ∈ X} =>
            SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id y.1) ⟨x, hx⟩
  /- Paper-facing curvature model from `#/assumptions/0/math`, quoting
     Eq. (4.2.2). -/
  hcurvature_model :
    ∀ x, x ∈ X → ∀ y, y ∈ X →
      mu * (nu y - nu x - ⟪gradNu x, y - x⟫_ℝ) ≤
        SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id y -
            SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id x -
            ⟪gradF x, y - x⟫_ℝ ∧
      SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id y -
          SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id x -
          ⟪gradF x, y - x⟫_ℝ ≤
        (L / 2) * ‖y - x‖ ^ 2 + M * ‖y - x‖
  /- Paper-facing stochastic oracle assumptions from `#/assumptions/1/math` and
     `#/assumptions/2/math`, packaged as one SFO object: unbiasedness, bounded
     variance, and the SOptLib joint residual measurability required to sample
     that same oracle at random feasible queries. -/
  stochasticOracle :
    AcsStochasticOracle (E := E) (S := S) X sampleMeasure gradL gradF sigma
  /- Paper setup datum for `#/setup/problem/math`, quoting
     `Ψ* := min_{x∈X} Ψ(x)`: a selected optimizer realizing the minimum.
     The value `Ψ*` below is defined from this optimizer rather than carried
     separately. -/
  xStar : E
  hxStar_mem : xStar ∈ X
  hxStar_minimizes :
    ∀ z, z ∈ X →
      SOptLib.compositeObjective
          (fun x => SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id x) h xStar ≤
        SOptLib.compositeObjective
          (fun x => SOptLib.objectiveExpectation sampleMeasure oracleValue id x) h z

/-- Lean realization of the paper stochastic sample stream used by AC-SA.

The paper setup only specifies the law of `ξ` and IID calls `ξ_t`; this wrapper
realizes those calls on an explicit probability space and supplies the
measurability/independence facts needed to construct filtrations and recursive
processes. -/
structure StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentRealization
    (setup : StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup E S)
    (Ω : Type*) [MeasurableSpace Ω] where
  xi : ℕ → Ω → S
  P : Measure Ω
  hP : IsProbabilityMeasure P
  hxi_meas : ∀ t, Measurable (xi t)
  hxi_indep : iIndepFun (β := fun _ => S) xi P
  hxi_law : ∀ t, Measure.map (xi t) P = setup.sampleMeasure

namespace StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup

variable (setup : StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup E S)

/-- Fixed-query unbiasedness projection from the packaged AC-SA SFO object. -/
theorem hgradL_unbiased :
    ∀ x, x ∈ setup.X →
      SOptLib.oracleWellDefined setup.sampleMeasure setup.gradL id x ∧
        SOptLib.oracleMean setup.sampleMeasure setup.gradL id x = setup.gradF x :=
  setup.stochasticOracle.unbiased

/-- Fixed-query variance projection from the packaged AC-SA SFO object. -/
theorem hgradL_variance :
    ∀ x, x ∈ setup.X →
      SOptLib.expectationWellDefined setup.sampleMeasure
          (fun ξ => SOptLib.dualNorm (setup.gradL x ξ - setup.gradF x) ^ 2) ∧
        SOptLib.expectation setup.sampleMeasure
            (fun ξ => SOptLib.dualNorm (setup.gradL x ξ - setup.gradF x) ^ 2) ≤
          setup.sigma ^ 2 :=
  setup.stochasticOracle.variance

/-- Expected stochastic objective `f(x) = E[F(x, ξ)]` from Eq. (4.2.1).
This specializes `SOptLib.objectiveExpectation` to the AC-SA sample process
rather than storing `f` as a separate witness field. -/
noncomputable def f (x : E) : ℝ :=
  SOptLib.objectiveExpectation setup.sampleMeasure setup.oracleValue id x

/-- Well-definedness of the paper objective expectation `f(x) = E[F(x, ξ)]`
on the feasible set, using `SOptLib.objectiveWellDefined`.

This is the semantic boundary for Eq. (4.2.1): the later proofs may unfold the
Bochner integral, but the paper-facing objective is only meaningful at feasible
points carrying this integrability certificate. -/
theorem objectiveWellDefined
    (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    SOptLib.objectiveWellDefined setup.sampleMeasure setup.oracleValue id x := by
  exact setup.hobjective_wellDefined x hx

/-- The paper objective expectation is definitionally the canonical `f`. -/
theorem objectiveExpectation_eq_f (x : E) :
    ∫ ξ, setup.oracleValue x ξ ∂setup.sampleMeasure = setup.f x := by
  rfl

/-- Well-definedness of the stochastic oracle mean in Eq. (4.1.3). -/
theorem oracleWellDefined
    (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    SOptLib.oracleWellDefined setup.sampleMeasure setup.gradL id x := by
  exact (setup.hgradL_unbiased x hx).1

/-- Canonical AC-SA stochastic gradient oracle `G(x, ξ)` from Eq. (4.1.3).

Aligns with `SOptLib.oracleKernel`: the SOptLib candidate supplies the
sample-composition primitive, and this setup-specialized definition fixes the
base sample map to the identity so the paper oracle is a function of the
source sample `ξ` rather than a free realized process. -/
def stochasticGradientOracle (x : E) : S → E :=
  SOptLib.oracleKernel setup.gradL (fun ξ : S => ξ) x

/-- Defining formula for the paper stochastic gradient oracle `G(x, ξ)`. -/
theorem stochasticGradientOracle_apply (x : E) (ξ : S) :
    setup.stochasticGradientOracle x ξ = setup.gradL x ξ := by
  rfl

/-- Mean-oracle form of the unbiasedness assumption
`E[G(x, ξ_t)] = f'(x)` from Eq. (4.1.3). -/
theorem oracleMean_eq_gradF (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    SOptLib.oracleMean setup.sampleMeasure setup.stochasticGradientOracle id x =
      setup.gradF x := by
  simpa [stochasticGradientOracle] using (setup.hgradL_unbiased x hx).2

/-- Pointwise oracle noise `G(x, ξ) - f'(x)`.

No SOptLib match: searched `oracle noise deviation delta`, checked
`SOptLib.oracleVarianceKernel` and `SOptLib.oracleDeviation_measurable`; those
candidates respectively package squared deviation from an oracle mean and
measurability of an already formed deviation, while Eq. (4.2.8) needs the
paper's pointwise vector `G(x, ξ) - f'(x)` relative to the named subgradient
`f'(x)`. -/
def oracleNoise (x : E) (ξ : S) : E :=
  setup.stochasticGradientOracle x ξ - setup.gradF x

/-- Defining formula for the fixed-query oracle noise. -/
theorem oracleNoise_apply (x : E) (ξ : S) :
    setup.oracleNoise x ξ = setup.gradL x ξ - setup.gradF x := by
  rfl

/-- Fixed-query centering of the paper oracle noise, derived from unbiasedness. -/
theorem oracleNoise_integral_eq_zero (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    ∫ ξ, setup.oracleNoise x ξ ∂setup.sampleMeasure = 0 := by
  letI : IsProbabilityMeasure setup.sampleMeasure := setup.hsampleMeasure
  exact integral_oracle_deviation_eq_zero_of_oracleMean_eq
    (μ := setup.sampleMeasure)
    (G := setup.stochasticGradientOracle)
    (target := setup.gradF)
    (x := x)
    (by
      simpa [stochasticGradientOracle, SOptLib.oracleKernel] using
        (setup.oracleWellDefined x hx))
    (setup.oracleMean_eq_gradF x hx)

/-- Well-definedness of the bounded-variance expectation in Assumption 3. -/
theorem oracleVarianceWellDefined
    (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    SOptLib.expectationWellDefined setup.sampleMeasure
      (fun ξ => SOptLib.dualNorm (setup.oracleNoise x ξ) ^ 2) := by
  simpa [oracleNoise, stochasticGradientOracle, SOptLib.dualNorm] using
    (setup.hgradL_variance x hx).1

/-- Bounded-variance assumption stated through the paper dual norm and the
canonical expectation wrapper, so the source-facing object is tied to the
well-defined expectation asserted by the oracle assumption. -/
theorem oracleVariance_bound (x : E) (hx : x ∈ setup.X) :
    SOptLib.expectation setup.sampleMeasure
        (fun ξ => SOptLib.dualNorm (setup.oracleNoise x ξ) ^ 2) ≤
      setup.sigma ^ 2 := by
  simpa [oracleNoise, stochasticGradientOracle, SOptLib.dualNorm] using
    (setup.hgradL_variance x hx).2

/-- Continuity on the carrier of the expected stochastic objective `f`.

This is derived from the source curvature model and the selected subgradient
field: at each feasible `x`, the subgradient inequality gives a continuous
affine lower support, while Eq. (4.2.2) gives a continuous quadratic upper model.
The two models squeeze `f` at `x` along the carrier. -/
theorem f_continuousOn :
    ContinuousOn setup.f setup.X := by
  classical
  intro x hx
  let lower : E → ℝ :=
    fun y => setup.f x + ⟪setup.gradF x, y - x⟫_ℝ
  let upper : E → ℝ :=
    fun y =>
      setup.f x + ⟪setup.gradF x, y - x⟫_ℝ +
        (setup.L / 2) * ‖y - x‖ ^ 2 + setup.M * ‖y - x‖
  have hlower_tendsto :
      Tendsto lower (𝓝[setup.X] x) (𝓝 (setup.f x)) := by
    have hcont : Continuous lower := by
      fun_prop
    have hxval : lower x = setup.f x := by
      simp [lower]
    have ht : Tendsto lower (𝓝 x) (𝓝 (lower x)) :=
      hcont.continuousAt.tendsto
    simpa [hxval] using ht.mono_left nhdsWithin_le_nhds
  have hupper_tendsto :
      Tendsto upper (𝓝[setup.X] x) (𝓝 (setup.f x)) := by
    have hcont : Continuous upper := by
      fun_prop
    have hxval : upper x = setup.f x := by
      simp [upper]
    have ht : Tendsto upper (𝓝 x) (𝓝 (upper x)) :=
      hcont.continuousAt.tendsto
    simpa [hxval] using ht.mono_left nhdsWithin_le_nhds
  have hlower_le :
      ∀ᶠ y in 𝓝[setup.X] x, lower y ≤ setup.f y := by
    filter_upwards [self_mem_nhdsWithin] with y hy
    have hs :=
      (SOptLib.mem_carrierSubdifferential_iff.mp
        (setup.hgradF_subgrad x hx)) ⟨y, hy⟩
    simpa [lower, StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.f] using hs
  have hle_upper :
      ∀ᶠ y in 𝓝[setup.X] x, setup.f y ≤ upper y := by
    filter_upwards [self_mem_nhdsWithin] with y hy
    have hcurv :
        setup.f y - setup.f x - ⟪setup.gradF x, y - x⟫_ℝ ≤
          setup.L / 2 * ‖y - x‖ ^ 2 + setup.M * ‖y - x‖ := by
      simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.f] using
        (setup.hcurvature_model x hx y hy).2
    dsimp [upper] at *
    linarith
  exact
    tendsto_of_tendsto_of_tendsto_of_le_of_le' hlower_tendsto hupper_tendsto
      hlower_le hle_upper

/-- Composite objective `Ψ(x) = f(x) + h(x)`. -/
noncomputable def Psi (x : E) : ℝ :=
  SOptLib.compositeObjective setup.f setup.h x

/-- Feasible-carrier form of the composite objective used in the paper's
definition `Ψ* := min_{x ∈ X} Ψ(x)`.

Aligns with `SOptLib.compositeObjective`; the carrier wrapper keeps the minimum
over `X` rather than over the ambient space. -/
noncomputable def PsiCarrier (x : {x : E // x ∈ setup.X}) : ℝ :=
  setup.Psi x.1

/-- Carrier measurability of the simple convex term.

This is a source-faithfulness boundary, not a consequence of the Lean
`knownStructure` encoding. -/
theorem hCarrier_measurable_from_simple :
    Measurable (fun x : {x : E // x ∈ setup.X} => setup.h x.1) := by
  exact IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm.carrier_measurable setup.hh_simple

/-- Carrier measurability of the expected stochastic objective `f`, derived from
the source curvature and subgradient assumptions. -/
theorem fCarrier_measurable_from_curvature :
    Measurable (fun x : {x : E // x ∈ setup.X} => setup.f x.1) := by
  have hcont :
      Continuous (fun x : {x : E // x ∈ setup.X} => setup.f x.1) :=
    continuous_subtype_of_continuousOn_ambient
      (X := setup.X)
      (f := fun x : {x : E // x ∈ setup.X} => setup.f x.1)
      (F := setup.f)
      setup.f_continuousOn
      (by intro x; rfl)
  exact hcont.measurable

/-- Producer for the carrier measurability required by the generated Theorem 4.4
route.

The `h` side depends on the explicit regularity leaf above; it is intentionally
not obtained by upgrading `simple convex` to Lipschitz/measurable structure. -/
theorem PsiCarrier_measurable_from_simple :
    Measurable setup.PsiCarrier := by
  simpa [PsiCarrier, Psi] using
    SOptLib.carrierCompositeObjective_measurable
      (X := setup.X) (f := setup.f) (h := setup.h)
      setup.fCarrier_measurable_from_curvature
      setup.hCarrier_measurable_from_simple

private theorem PsiCarrier_convexOn_totalize :
    ConvexOn ℝ setup.X (SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier) := by
  refine ⟨setup.hX_convex, ?_⟩
  intro x hx y hy a b ha hb hab
  let w : E := a • x + b • y
  have hw : w ∈ setup.X := by
    simpa [w] using setup.hX_convex hx hy ha hb hab
  have hf_support_x :
      setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ ≤ setup.f x := by
    have hs :=
      (SOptLib.mem_carrierSubdifferential_iff.mp
        (setup.hgradF_subgrad w hw)) ⟨x, hx⟩
    simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.f] using hs
  have hf_support_y :
      setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ ≤ setup.f y := by
    have hs :=
      (SOptLib.mem_carrierSubdifferential_iff.mp
        (setup.hgradF_subgrad w hw)) ⟨y, hy⟩
    simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.f] using hs
  have hinner_zero :
      a * ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ +
        b * ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ = 0 := by
    have hvec :
        a • (x - w) + b • (y - w) = (0 : E) := by
      calc
        a • (x - w) + b • (y - w)
            = a • x + b • y - (a + b) • w := by
                module
        _ = w - (a + b) • w := by
                simp [w]
        _ = w - (1 : ℝ) • w := by
                rw [hab]
        _ = 0 := by
                simp
    have hinner :
        ⟪setup.gradF w, a • (x - w) + b • (y - w)⟫_ℝ = 0 := by
      rw [hvec, inner_zero_right]
    simpa [inner_add_right, real_inner_smul_right] using hinner
  have hf_conv :
      setup.f w ≤ a * setup.f x + b * setup.f y := by
    have hxscaled :
        a * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ) ≤
          a * setup.f x :=
      mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hf_support_x ha
    have hyscaled :
        b * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ) ≤
          b * setup.f y :=
      mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hf_support_y hb
    have hsum_scaled :
        a * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ) +
            b * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ) ≤
          a * setup.f x + b * setup.f y := by
      linarith
    have hleft_eq :
        a * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ) +
            b * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ) =
          setup.f w := by
      calc
        a * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ) +
            b * (setup.f w + ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ)
            = (a + b) * setup.f w +
                (a * ⟪setup.gradF w, x - w⟫_ℝ +
                  b * ⟪setup.gradF w, y - w⟫_ℝ) := by
                ring
        _ = setup.f w := by
                rw [hinner_zero, hab]
                ring
    exact hleft_eq ▸ hsum_scaled
  have hh_conv :
      setup.h w ≤ a * setup.h x + b * setup.h y := by
    have hh :=
      (IsAcsSimpleConvexTerm.convex setup.hh_simple).2 hx hy ha hb hab
    simpa [w, smul_eq_mul] using hh
  have htotal_w :
      SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier w = setup.Psi w := by
    simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.PsiCarrier] using
      (SOptLib.totalizeOn_of_mem setup.X setup.PsiCarrier hw)
  have htotal_x :
      SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier x = setup.Psi x := by
    simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.PsiCarrier] using
      (SOptLib.totalizeOn_of_mem setup.X setup.PsiCarrier hx)
  have htotal_y :
      SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier y = setup.Psi y := by
    simpa [StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.PsiCarrier] using
      (SOptLib.totalizeOn_of_mem setup.X setup.PsiCarrier hy)
  calc
    SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier (a • x + b • y)
        = setup.f w + setup.h w := by
            simp [w, htotal_w, StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.Psi,
              SOptLib.compositeObjective]
    _ ≤ a * setup.f x + b * setup.f y +
          (a * setup.h x + b * setup.h y) := by
            nlinarith
    _ = a • SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier x +
          b • SOptLib.totalizeOn setup.X setup.PsiCarrier y := by
            simp [htotal_x, htotal_y, StochasticAcceleratedGradientDescentSetup.Psi,
              SOptLib.compositeObjective, smul_eq_mul]
            ring

/-- Subtype Borel sublevel measurability for a closed finite-dimensional convex
carrier objective.

This is the exact remaining infrastructure leaf behind
`carrierConvexOn_measurable_of_closed`: prove
`{x : X | φ x ≤ r}` is Borel in the carrier subtype from closedness of `X` and
convexity of the canonical totalization. The available SOptLib Haar-volume
null-measurability theorem is relevant evidence but is not by itself sufficient
for this Borel/subtype target. -/
private theorem _voucher_step_carrierConvexOn_ambient_sublevel_nullMeasurable_29
    {X : Set E} {φ : {x : E // x ∈ X} → ℝ}
    (hconv : ConvexOn ℝ X (SOptLib.totalizeOn X φ))
    (r : ℝ) :
    NullMeasurableSet
      {x : E | x ∈ X ∧ SOptLib.totalizeOn X φ x ≤ r}
      (volume : Measure E) := by
  exact SOptLib.carrierConvexOn_sublevel_nullMeasurable_volume
    (X := X) φ r hconv

private theorem _voucher_step_carrierConvexOn_ambient_sublevel_convex_32
    {X : Set E} {φ : {x : E // x ∈ X} → ℝ}
    (hconv : ConvexOn ℝ X (SOptLib.totalizeOn X φ))
